= The Left Revolts

Béla Kun has gone back to Moscow, and his report has gone ahead of him: the German Zentrale refused to fight when the enemy attacked. Ruth Fischer and Arkadi Maslow have carried a resolution through the Berlin district conference condemning the "passivity" of the leadership. Hamburg has followed. Halle-Merseburg, left alone against Hörsing's police, is bitter.

But the party has kept its members, its presses and its people in the unions. In the factories, social democratic workers admit that the communists did not walk into the trap.

- @stand: Stand by the decision.
- @seats: Give the Left seats in the Zentrale.

@stand
subtitle: The Left will carry its grievance to Moscow. The USPD will like it.
on-arrival: independence += 5; offensive_satisfaction -= 10; uspd_relation += 5; lean_luxemburgist += 2

The Zentrale publishes a long statement: a party that lets its enemies choose the day of battle will lose every battle. The USPD's paper reprints it.

@seats
subtitle: The Left becomes the junior partner. Every line will have to be agreed with Berlin.
on-arrival: {!
Q.junior_faction = 'offensive';
Q.offensive_satisfaction += 15;
Q.lean_offensive += 1;
RG.setRole('fischer', 'Zentrale; leader of the Left');
!}

Ruth Fischer and two of her allies join the Zentrale. The first meeting lasts until three in the morning.
